# Copy to infra/.env.prod on the deploy host and fill in. PLACEHOLDERS ONLY in
# this file — infra/.env.prod is gitignored and is the only place real values
# live.
#
#   docker compose -f infra/docker-compose.yml -f infra/docker-compose.prod.yml \
#     --env-file infra/.env.prod up -d
#
# Variables marked REQUIRED have no default: the compose file uses `:?`, so a
# missing one fails the `up` immediately rather than starting a half-configured
# stack. Everything not listed here keeps the default in
# infra/docker-compose.yml.
#
# `docker compose ... config` renders this file in full, secrets included. Do
# not paste its output anywhere.

# =====================================================================
# 1 · IDENTITY — one origin, three variables, they move together
# =====================================================================
# This is the single decision the whole auth path hangs off, so it is one
# variable and the compose file derives the rest of it:
#
#   services/actors  BETTER_AUTH_URL  = PUBLIC_APP_ORIGIN
#   services/api     AUTH_ISSUER      = PUBLIC_APP_ORIGIN
#   services/api     AUTH_AUDIENCE    = PUBLIC_APP_ORIGIN
#
# It is the **public Next origin on Vercel** — the address a browser sees —
# and NOT this machine's edge hostname. The Next app proxies /api/auth/* and
# /graphql through to Loki (src/lib/api/auth-proxy.ts), so the browser and the
# OAuth providers only ever talk to Vercel. Setting this to PUBLIC_EDGE_HOST
# would send OAuth callbacks straight past the proxy and set session cookies on
# an origin the app never reads.
#
# Four things derive from it, all verified against better-auth 1.7.3's source:
# the OAuth redirect URIs (section 2), the JWT `iss` and `aud` (which services/api
# compares as *strings* — a trailing slash is a mismatch), the `__Secure-`
# cookie prefix, and an implicitly trusted request origin.
#
# Both apps assert this on startup: `assertPublicAuthOrigins`
# (services/actors/src/auth/config.ts) and `assertAuthIdentityCoherence`
# (services/api/src/config.ts). https, no trailing slash, no path. REQUIRED.
PUBLIC_APP_ORIGIN=https://cellar.example.com

# Session and JWKS encryption. Generate once, keep it: rotating it invalidates
# every session and every signing key. REQUIRED.
#   openssl rand -base64 32
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=

# What the Next proxy on Vercel proves itself to the actor host with; only then
# is its x-cellar-client-ip trusted, so better-auth's rate limit counts each
# user and not Vercel's shared egress address. Set the SAME value in the Vercel
# project's environment (deploy-loki.md §2.5). At least 32 characters. REQUIRED.
#   openssl rand -hex 32
AUTH_PROXY_SECRET=

# Extra origins allowed to start an auth flow, comma-separated. PUBLIC_APP_ORIGIN
# is trusted implicitly, so this is normally empty — it is for Vercel preview
# deployments. A loopback entry here is rejected at startup in production:
# better-auth's origin check is a CSRF defence, and trusting http://localhost
# lets any page on a visitor's own machine drive their session.
AUTH_TRUSTED_ORIGINS=

# Where services/api fetches the key set. Service-internal on purpose: routing it
# over the public edge would make the API's startup depend on DNS, TLS and the
# router. Leave it unless the topology changes.
AUTH_JWKS_URL=http://actors:3002/api/auth/jwks

# Dapr API tokens (docs/architecture/target-stack.md, "Dapr API tokens").
# Both REQUIRED, both generated, both distinct from each other and from the
# published development defaults in infra/docker-compose.yml. The deploy
# refuses a published default here, and for MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD and
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD too — any secret-named variable
# equal to a value the base compose file falls back to
# (scripts/deploy/check-prod-config.mjs; docs/architecture/deploy-loki.md §3).
#
# DAPR_API_TOKEN — every caller of a sidecar's API must present it
# (`dapr-api-token`); daprd answers 401 otherwise. Given to both apps and
# both sidecars.
#   openssl rand -hex 32
DAPR_API_TOKEN=
# APP_API_TOKEN — what each sidecar presents to its app; the actors app
# refuses /actors/* and /dapr/* without it, so nothing else on the compose
# network can call an actor as `system`. Given to the actors app and both
# sidecars. Rotating either means restarting the apps AND the sidecars.
#   openssl rand -hex 32
APP_API_TOKEN=

# =====================================================================
# 2 · OAUTH — register these exact redirect URIs before first sign-in
# =====================================================================
# `<PUBLIC_APP_ORIGIN>/api/auth/callback/<provider>`. Derived, not guessed:
# better-auth builds `${context.baseURL}/callback/${provider.id}`
# (dist/api/routes/sign-in.mjs), `context.baseURL` is BETTER_AUTH_URL + the
# `/api/auth` base path (dist/utils/url.mjs), and none of these three providers
# overrides `callbackPath`. With the example origin above:
#
#   Google    https://cellar.example.com/api/auth/callback/google
#   Facebook  https://cellar.example.com/api/auth/callback/facebook
#   Discord   https://cellar.example.com/api/auth/callback/discord
#
# Print them for your own origin:
#   cd services/actors && node -e 'import("./src/auth/config.ts").then(m => \
#     console.log(m.oauthRedirectUris(process.env.PUBLIC_APP_ORIGIN)))'
#
# A provider is enabled only when BOTH halves are non-empty; leave a pair blank
# to leave that provider off. PLACEHOLDERS ONLY.
GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
FACEBOOK_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
FACEBOOK_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
DISCORD_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
DISCORD_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=

# =====================================================================
# 3 · PUBLIC EDGE — two hostnames on the host's existing nginx-proxy
# =====================================================================
# This stack does not run its own edge. Loki's `nginx-proxy` (with
# `nginx-proxy-acme`) already owns 80/443 for other services on the host; the
# api, actors and minio containers carry VIRTUAL_HOST / LETSENCRYPT_HOST for the
# two names below, scripts/deploy/edge.sh attaches them to the proxy's network
# and installs their nginx config, and acme-companion issues the certificates
# with the Let's Encrypt account it already has (its DEFAULT_EMAIL — there is
# no ACME address to set here any more). Both names need public DNS pointing
# at this host, and an existing TCP 80 + 443 forward to Loki. See
# docs/architecture/deploy-loki.md §2.

# Serves /graphql, /healthz and /api/auth/* only; every other path is a 404.
# The Next app on Vercel is its only client; browsers never address it
# directly. REQUIRED.
PUBLIC_EDGE_HOST=loki.example.com

# MinIO's S3 API, on a hostname of its own — NOT a path on the edge above.
# SigV4 signs both the Host header and the URI path, and the upload signer
# produces path-style `/<bucket>/<key>` URLs, so a `/files` prefix would break
# every signature twice over. REQUIRED.
PUBLIC_FILES_HOST=files.example.com

# First-deploy rehearsal against Let's Encrypt *staging* (far looser rate
# limits): "true" sets LETSENCRYPT_TEST on this stack's three containers only,
# so acme-companion issues untrusted staging certificates for these two names
# and leaves every other certificate on the host alone. Untrusted in a browser
# is how you know you are still on staging. Set back to false (or delete the
# line) and `up -d` for real certificates — deploy-loki.md §4.
ACME_STAGING=false

# services/api's loopback port, for a curl on the host during a deploy.
# ⚠ On Loki the default 3001 is already taken by another service, and the
# `up` then fails with "address already in use". Pick a free one (`ss -ltn`).
API_PORT=3011

# =====================================================================
# 4 · FILES — the signed hostname
# =====================================================================
#   FILES_S3_ENDPOINT   must equal PUBLIC_FILES_HOST, as a BARE HOSTNAME.
#                       services/actors/src/lib/s3-presign.ts concatenates it
#                       into the signed Host header, so `filesS3Config` throws
#                       at startup on a scheme, a port or a path.
#
# FILES_S3_USE_SSL is pinned to "true" in the compose file and FILES_S3_PORT to
# empty, so the signer defaults to 443 and signs a bare `files.example.com` —
# which is exactly the Host nginx-proxy forwards to MinIO. There is
# deliberately no FILES_S3_PORT line below; `:port` inside the signature would
# break it.
#
# FILES_S3_PUBLIC_URL is no longer read by this stack: it fed a production
# override of the Dapr `files` binding that pointed the sidecars at the public
# hostname through Caddy. The sidecars now use MinIO in-network
# (infra/docker-compose.prod.yml, header note 5). Vercel still accepts it as the
# other spelling of PUBLIC_FILES_HOST (deploy-loki.md §2.5) — set it there, not
# here.
FILES_S3_ENDPOINT=files.example.com

# Bucket name. Must match `DEFAULT_BUCKET` in s3-presign.ts and the literal in
# the Dapr binding; changing it means changing all three.
MINIO_BUCKET=cellar-files

# MinIO's root credentials — used by the binding's sidecar, by the in-process
# upload signer and by the bucket bootstrap. REQUIRED.
#   openssl rand -base64 24
MINIO_ROOT_USER=
MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD=

# =====================================================================
# 5 · DATABASE
# =====================================================================
# services/actors is the only process given these (target-stack §1); services/api
# refuses to start if any of them reaches it. REQUIRED.
#   openssl rand -base64 24
POSTGRES_USER=cellar
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
POSTGRES_DB=cellar
# Published on 127.0.0.1 only, for pg_dump and psql on this host.
POSTGRES_PORT=5433

# =====================================================================
# 6 · OBSERVABILITY — LAN only
# =====================================================================
# The LAN address of this machine (<loki-lan-ip>; the value below is only an
# example of the shape — replace it with your own). Grafana's :3010 is
# published on this interface and nothing else, which is what keeps the
# dashboard off the WAN; the default (127.0.0.1) makes it reachable only from
# the box itself. Find it with `ip -4 addr` / `ipconfig getifaddr en0`.
LAN_BIND_ADDR=192.168.1.10

# Grafana's admin password — the only way in: anonymous access is off in the
# prod overlay (the image's default is anonymous ADMIN). REQUIRED.
#   openssl rand -base64 24
#
# ⚠ READ ONCE, when Grafana creates its database on the first start. Grafana's
# data lives on the otel-lgtm-data volume, so editing this line later changes
# nothing by itself. To rotate: edit it here, `up -d otel-lgtm`, then copy it
# into the database — docs/architecture/deploy-loki.md §9.2 has the one-liner.
# Five wrong logins in five minutes lock the admin account for five minutes.
GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# What Grafana believes its own address is, for generated links.
GRAFANA_ROOT_URL=http://192.168.1.10:3010/

# Alerts: no variable. They are visible in Grafana's Alerting page on the LAN
# and delivered nowhere (infra/grafana/provisioning/alerting/).

# =====================================================================
# 7 · IMAGES
# =====================================================================
# Built ON LOKI from a commit, by scripts/deploy/pull-deploy.sh, tagged
# <project>-api:<sha8> / <project>-actors:<sha8>. The deployer exports the
# running release's pair from ~/cellar-prod/state/release.env into every compose
# call, which beats this file — so these two are what a compose command WITHOUT
# that env file gets, i.e. the first, hand-made deploy (deploy-loki.md §4).
# Pin to an immutable tag (a commit sha) rather than `latest`. REQUIRED.
API_IMAGE=cellar-prod-api:00000000
ACTORS_IMAGE=cellar-prod-actors:00000000

# Third-party images. The object store is pgsty/silo, a maintained MinIO fork
# that uses the same on-disk format, and `minio-init` runs its client,
# pgsty/mc. Both are PINNED by multi-arch digest in infra/docker-compose.yml.
# The upstream `minio/minio` and `minio/mc` images were deleted from Docker Hub
# in 2026-09. To override either, set an immutable digest, never a floating
# tag. otel-lgtm is also PINNED (0.32.1) in the compose files, because its
# data outlives the container. Override it only together with the upgrade
# checklist in docs/architecture/deploy-loki.md §9.5 (the Prometheus config
# override is a copy of that exact tag's file).
# MINIO_IMAGE=docker.io/pgsty/silo:RELEASE.2026-09-16T00-00-00Z@sha256:635197cb9f36d01bee221d34d1c7d7960f6a95c48b0b6c01d99cd13bdae51a46
# MC_IMAGE=docker.io/pgsty/mc:RELEASE.2026-09-16T00-00-00Z@sha256:cfc83108c3abb371f8fb84d99c1fdc88f8c237e022409b0081fb7c0a3be634dd
# OTEL_LGTM_IMAGE=grafana/otel-lgtm:0.32.1

# =====================================================================
# 8 · AI PROVIDER
# =====================================================================
# Production runs the Gemini models on VERTEX AI (docs/architecture/deploy-loki.md
# §2.7, settled in e4-decisions.md §12); Loki serves no model. infra/.env.example
# has the full explanation of each variable. This needs a GCP project and a
# service account with roles/aiplatform.user — the key is read from the
# environment only, inline JSON or a file path, never from the database.
# A provider that is set but incomplete makes the actor host refuse to start;
# unset AI_PROVIDER entirely to deploy with no model (every AI feature then
# fails with an error naming itself, which is supported).
AI_PROVIDER=vertex-ai
GOOGLE_GCP_PROJECT_ID=
# The chat models (gemini-3.x) are served from the `global` endpoint.
GOOGLE_GCP_LOCATION=global
# Leave EMPTY. Empty reads as unset, and unset sends gemini-embedding-2 to
# `global` — the ONLY location Vertex serves it from. A region here (the old
# us-central1) 404s every embedding call, and the actor host refuses to boot
# with one pinned (services/actors/src/lib/ai/config.ts).
VERTEX_AI_EMBEDDING_LOCATION=
# One of the two: the key inline (what a secrets manager hands you) or a path
# to it inside the actors container.
GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS_JSON=
#GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS=/run/secrets/vertex-key.json
AI_EMBEDDING_DIMENSIONS=768
AI_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS=120000
# Model overrides — optional, empty means the code's defaults
# (gemini-3.1-flash-lite / gemini-3.5-flash-lite / gemini-3.5-flash, embeddings
# gemini-embedding-2; config.ts GEMINI_CHAT_MODELS). Never pin a gemini-2.5-*:
# Vertex retires them on 2026-10-20.
# Changing the embedding model is a full re-embed (deploy-loki.md §4.2).
#VERTEX_AI_MODEL_LOW=
#VERTEX_AI_MODEL_MEDIUM=
#VERTEX_AI_MODEL_HIGH=
#VERTEX_AI_EMBEDDING_MODEL=

# Fallback only, if the GCP account is not ready on the day (deploy-loki.md
# §2.7): ollama on the HOST, reached at host.docker.internal, with the model
# pulls done on this machine. Switching provider later is a re-embed.
#AI_PROVIDER=ollama
#OLLAMA_ENDPOINT=http://host.docker.internal:11434
#OLLAMA_EMBEDDING_MODEL=nomic-embed-text
#OLLAMA_MODEL_LOW=gemma3:4b
#OLLAMA_MODEL_MEDIUM=gemma3:4b
#OLLAMA_MODEL_HIGH=gemma3:4b

# BudgetActor overrides — optional, empty means none. Formats and the seam list
# are in infra/.env.example ("AI spend overrides"); a malformed value makes
# BudgetActor refuse to load rather than meter at list price.
#AI_MODEL_PRICES=gemini-3.5-flash-lite=30000000/250000000
#AI_BUDGET_MAX_REQUESTS=embedding=50000
#BUDGET_USER_CAPS=ai_model/embedding=600/4000

# google-ai (an AI Studio key) is not the deployed provider; listed so the
# name is known. Only read when AI_PROVIDER=google-ai.
#GOOGLE_AI_API_KEY=
